# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: `EventProcessor`

## Critical Breaking Changes

| Issue | .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|-------|---------------|--------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `ThreadAbortException` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Wakes blocking calls | Still works, but discouraged |
| Manual threading + locks | Common pattern | Replaced by `Channel<T>`, async/await |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` | Manual synchronization | `Channel<T>` handles both |

## Race Condition in Original Code

The original has a bug: if multiple items are enqueued before `WaitOne` returns, `Reset()` clears the signal and only **one** item is dequeued — the rest are stranded until the next enqueue.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _runTask;
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        // ChannelReader.ReadAllAsync handles backpressure and
        // cancellation; no manual synchronization needed.
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        // TryWrite on an unbounded channel always succeeds
        // unless the channel is completed.
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Processor has been stopped.");
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        // 1. Signal no more writes — Reader loop will end naturally.
        _channel.Writer.Complete();

        // 2. Give the loop time to drain, then force-cancel.
        if (_runTask is not null)
        {
            using var timeout = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            try
            {
                await _runTask.WaitAsync(timeout.Token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException)
            {
                // Drain timed out — cancel the read loop.
                await _cts.CancelAsync();
            }
        }

        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // your processing logic
    }
}
```

## Key Migration Pitfalls

1. **`Thread.Abort()` is gone** — the most common migration failure. Use `CancellationToken` for cooperative cancellation instead.

2. **`Channel<T>` replaces three primitives at once** — the `Queue`, the `ManualResetEvent`, and the `lock`. Don't migrate them individually; replace the whole pattern.

3. **The race condition disappears** — `ReadAllAsync` drains all available items, unlike the original `WaitOne`/`Reset`/`Dequeue` sequence.

4. **Graceful shutdown is two-phase** — call `Writer.Complete()` to stop accepting items and let the loop finish, then use `CancellationToken` as a hard timeout. This replaces the `Interrupt` → `Join` → `Abort` ladder.

5. **`IAsyncDisposable`** — use `await using` at the call site so cleanup is awaited properly:
   ```csharp
   await using var processor = new EventProcessor();
   processor.Start();
   ```

6. **If `Handle` is async** — change the signature to `async Task HandleAsync(string item)` and `await` it inside the loop. The `Channel` pattern composes naturally with async, unlike the original thread-based approach.
